vernance of the District, creating a new state government and redefining the federal district."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Statehood would change property tax authority and land-use regulation for most of the current District, affecting developers and property owners."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The transition of DC courts, judicial proceedings, and contractual obligations to state authority would affect legal practice and litigation in the new jurisdiction."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would admit most of the current District of Columbia as the 51st state, called Washington, Douglass Commonwealth, with two senators and one representative in Congress. A small federal district containing the Capitol, White House, Supreme Court, and nearby monuments would remain as the seat of the federal government. The bill sets up a transition process for DC laws, courts, contracts, and obligations to continue under state authority, and creates a commission to manage the shift to statehood.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who votes.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001245","score":0.050448841212731466},"overlapCount":197},"meaning":{"percentile":56.926235981677465,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[{"kind":"state-industry","text":"Lawyers & Lobbyists is also the top donor sector for 2 other members from CA. That points to the industry being big in the state, not to anything about this member."}],"unattributedShare":0.5381160539214825}},{"billId":"119-hr-1810","bioguideId":"A000371","cycle":2026,"score":0.0010882555406090048,"matches":[{"industry":"education","donorAmount":3500,"donorShare":0.0010882555406090048,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0010882555406090048}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted shar